{"tool_name":"ensembl","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"ensembl","home":"http://www.ensembl.org/","summary":"Genome databases for vertebrates genomes.","addition_date":"2015-06-11T13:36:34Z","last_update_date":"2021-04-17T20:18:33Z","tool_type":["Database portal","Web application"],"collections":["RD-connect","Rare Disease"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:ensembl","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"flicek@ebi.ac.uk","name":"Paul Flicek","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://www.ensembl.org/info/about/contact/index.html"},{"name":"EMBL-EBI Databases","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://www.ensembl.org/info/index.html"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["ELIXIR-EE"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Deposition","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3431"},{"term":"Genome annotation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0362"},{"term":"Genome visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3208"},{"term":"Query and retrieval","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0224"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Ensembl","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"EMBL-EBI_Databases","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/30.1.38","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org/) database project provides a bioinformatics framework to organise biology around the sequences of large genomes. It is a comprehensive source of stable automatic annotation of the human genome sequence, with confirmed gene predictions that have been integrated with external data sources, and is available as either an interactive web site or as flat files. It is also an open source software engineering project to develop a portable system able to handle very large genomes and associated requirements from sequence analysis to data storage and visualisation. The Ensembl site is one of the leading sources of human genome sequence annotation and provided much of the analysis for publication by the international human genome project of the draft genome. The Ensembl system is being installed around the world in both companies and academic sites on machines ranging from supercomputers to laptops.","authors":[{"name":"Barker D."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Cameron G."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clamp M."},{"name":"Clark L."},{"name":"Cox T."},{"name":"Cuff J."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Down T."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Eyras E."},{"name":"Gilbert J."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Hubbard T."},{"name":"Huminiecki L."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Lehvaslaiho H."},{"name":"Lijnzaad P."},{"name":"Melsopp C."},{"name":"Mongin E."},{"name":"Pettett R."},{"name":"Pocock M."},{"name":"Potter S."},{"name":"Rust A."},{"name":"Schmidt E."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spooner W."},{"name":"Stabenau A."},{"name":"Stalker J."},{"name":"Stupka E."},{"name":"Ureta-Vidal A."},{"name":"Vastrik I."}],"citationCount":1068,"date":"2002-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"The Ensembl genome database project"},"pmcid":"PMC99161","pmid":"11752248","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkg083","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org/) database project provides a bioinformatics framework to organise biology around the sequences of large genomes. It is a comprehensive source of stable automatic annotation of human, mouse and other genome sequences, available as either an interactive web site or as flat files. Ensembl also integrates manually annotated gene structures from external sources where available. As well as being one of the leading sources of genome annotation, Ensembl is an open source software engineering project to develop a portable system able to handle very large genomes and associated requirements. These range from sequence analysis to data storage and visualisation and installations exist around the world in both companies and at academic sites. With both human and mouse genome sequences available and more vertebrate sequences to follow, many of the recent developments in Ensembl have focusing on developing automatic comparative genome analysis and visualisation.","authors":[{"name":"Andrews D."},{"name":"Barker D."},{"name":"Bevan P."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Cameron G."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clamp M."},{"name":"Clark L."},{"name":"Cox T."},{"name":"Cuff J."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Down T."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Eyras E."},{"name":"Gilbert J."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Hubbard T."},{"name":"Iyer V."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Lehvaslaiho H."},{"name":"Melsopp C."},{"name":"Mongin E."},{"name":"Pettett R."},{"name":"Potter S."},{"name":"Rust A."},{"name":"Schmidt E."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spooner W."},{"name":"Stabenau A."},{"name":"Stalker J."},{"name":"Stupka E."},{"name":"Ureta-Vidal A."},{"name":"Vastrik I."}],"citationCount":189,"date":"2003-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Ensembl 2002: Accommodating comparative genomics"},"pmcid":"PMC165530","pmid":"12519943","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gki138","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org/) project provides a comprehensive and integrated source of annotation of large genome sequences. Over the last year the number of genomes available from the Ensembl site has increased by 7 to 16, with the addition of the six vertebrate genomes of chimpanzee, dog, cow, chicken, tetraodon and frog and the insect genome of honeybee. The majority have been annotated automatically using the Ensembl gene build system, showing its flexibility to reliably annotate a wide variety of genomes. With the increased number of vertebrate genomes, the comparative analysis provided to users has been greatly improved, with new website interfaces allowing annotation of different genomes to be directly compared. The Ensembl software system is being increasingly widely reused in different projects showing the benefits of a completely open approach to software development and distribution. \u00a9 Oxford University Press 2005; all rights reserved.","authors":[{"name":"Andrews D."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Caccamo M."},{"name":"Cameron G."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clamp M."},{"name":"Clarke L."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cox T."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Cutts T."},{"name":"Down T."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Fernandez-Suarez X.M."},{"name":"Gilbert J."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Herrero J."},{"name":"Hotz H."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Hubbard T."},{"name":"Iyer V."},{"name":"Jekosch K."},{"name":"Kahari A."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Keenan S."},{"name":"Kokocinsci F."},{"name":"London D."},{"name":"Longden I."},{"name":"McVicker G."},{"name":"Meidl P."},{"name":"Melsopp C."},{"name":"Potter S."},{"name":"Proctor G."},{"name":"Rae M."},{"name":"Rios D."},{"name":"Schuster M."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Severin J."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smedley D."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spooner W."},{"name":"Stabenau A."},{"name":"Stalker J."},{"name":"Storey R."},{"name":"Trevanion S."},{"name":"Ureta-Vidal A."},{"name":"Vogel J."},{"name":"White S."},{"name":"Woodwark C."}],"citationCount":327,"date":"2005-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Ensembl 2005"},"pmcid":"PMC540092","pmid":"15608235","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkj133","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org/) project provides a comprehensive and integrated source of annotation of large genome sequences. Over the last year the number of genomes available from the Ensembl site has increased from 4 to 19, with the addition of the mammalian genomes of Rhesus macaque and Opossum, the chordate genome of Ciona intestinalis and the import and integration of the yeast genome. The year has also seen extensive improvements to both data analysis and presentation, with the introduction of a redesigned website, the addition of RNA gene and regulatory annotation and substantial improvements to the integration of human genome variation data.","authors":[{"name":"Andrews D."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Caccamo M."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clarke L."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cox T."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Cutts T."},{"name":"Down T."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Fernandez-Suarez X.M."},{"name":"Flicek P."},{"name":"Graf S."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Herrero J."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Hubbard T.J."},{"name":"Iyer V."},{"name":"Jekosch K."},{"name":"Kahari A."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Kokocinski F."},{"name":"Kulesha E."},{"name":"London D."},{"name":"Longden I."},{"name":"Meidl P."},{"name":"Melsopp C."},{"name":"Overduin B."},{"name":"Parker A."},{"name":"Prlic A."},{"name":"Proctor G."},{"name":"Rae M."},{"name":"Redmond S."},{"name":"Rios D."},{"name":"Schuster M."},{"name":"Sealy I."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Severin J."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smedley D."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Stabenau A."},{"name":"Stalker J."},{"name":"Trevanion S."},{"name":"Ureta-Vidal A."},{"name":"Vogel J."},{"name":"White S."},{"name":"Woodwark C."}],"citationCount":280,"date":"2006-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic acids research","title":"Ensembl 2006."},"pmcid":"PMC1347495","pmid":"16381931","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkl996","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org/) project provides a comprehensive and integrated source of annotation of chordate genome sequences. Over the past year the number of genomes available from Ensembl has increased from 15 to 33, with the addition of sites for the mammalian genomes of elephant, rabbit, armadillo, tenrec, platypus, pig, cat, bush baby, common shrew, microbat and european hedgehog; the fish genomes of stickleback and medaka and the second example of the genomes of the sea squirt (Ciona savignyi) and the mosquito (Aedes aegypti). Some of the major features added during the year include the first complete gene sets for genomes with low-sequence coverage, the introduction of new strain variation data and the introduction of new orthology/paralog annotations based on gene trees. \u00a9 2007 Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Aken B.L."},{"name":"Ballester B."},{"name":"Beal K."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Caccamo M."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clarke L."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cox T."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Cutts T."},{"name":"Down T."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Dyer S.C."},{"name":"Fernandez-Banet J."},{"name":"Fernandez-Suarez X.M."},{"name":"Fitzgerald S."},{"name":"Flicek P."},{"name":"Graf S."},{"name":"Haider S."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Herrero J."},{"name":"Holland R."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Hubbard T.J.P."},{"name":"Johnson N."},{"name":"Kahari A."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Kokocinski F."},{"name":"Kulesha E."},{"name":"Lawson D."},{"name":"Longden I."},{"name":"Megy K."},{"name":"Meidl P."},{"name":"Melsopp C."},{"name":"Ouverdin B."},{"name":"Parker A."},{"name":"Prlic A."},{"name":"Proctor G."},{"name":"Rice S."},{"name":"Rios D."},{"name":"Schuster M."},{"name":"Sealy I."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Severin J."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smedley D."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spudich G."},{"name":"Trevanion S."},{"name":"Ureta-Vidal A."},{"name":"Vilella A."},{"name":"Vogel J."},{"name":"White S."},{"name":"Wood M."}],"citationCount":537,"date":"2007-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Ensembl 2007"},"pmcid":"PMC1761443","pmid":"17148474","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkm988","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl project (http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink) is a comprehensive genome information system featuring an integrated set of genome annotation, databases and other information for chordate and selected model organism and disease vector genomes. As of release 47 (October 2007), Ensembl fully supports 35 species, with preliminary support for six additional species. New species in the past year include platypus and horse. Major additions and improvements to Ensembl since our previous report include extensive support for functional genomics data in the form of a specialized functional genomics database, genome-wide maps of protein-DNA interactions and the Ensembl regulatory build; support for customization of the Ensembl web interface through the addition of user accounts and user groups; and increased support for genome resequencing. We have also introduced new comparative genomics-based data mining options and report on the continued development of our software infrastructure. \u00a9 2007 The Author(s).","authors":[{"name":"Aken B.L."},{"name":"Ballester B."},{"name":"Beal K."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Caccamo M."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clarke L."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cox T."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Cutts T."},{"name":"Down T."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Dyer S.C."},{"name":"Eyre T."},{"name":"Fernandez-Banet J."},{"name":"Fernandez-Suarez X.M."},{"name":"Fitzgerald S."},{"name":"Flicek P."},{"name":"Graf S."},{"name":"Haider S."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Herrero J."},{"name":"Holland R."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Howe K.L."},{"name":"Hubbard T.J.P."},{"name":"Jenkinson A."},{"name":"Johnson N."},{"name":"Kahari A."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Kokocinski F."},{"name":"Kulesha E."},{"name":"Lawson D."},{"name":"Longden L."},{"name":"Megy K."},{"name":"Meidl P."},{"name":"Overduin B."},{"name":"Parker A."},{"name":"Pritchard B."},{"name":"Prlic A."},{"name":"Proctor G."},{"name":"Rice S."},{"name":"Rios D."},{"name":"Schuster M."},{"name":"Sealy I."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smedley D."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spudich G."},{"name":"Trevanion S."},{"name":"Ureta-Vidal A."},{"name":"Vilella A.J."},{"name":"Vogel J."},{"name":"White S."},{"name":"Wood M."}],"citationCount":347,"date":"2008-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Ensembl 2008"},"pmcid":"PMC2238821","pmid":"18000006","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkn828","metadata":{"abstract":"The Ensembl project (http://www.ensembl.org) is a comprehensive genome information system featuring an integrated set of genome annotation, databases, and other information for chordate, selected model organism and disease vector genomes. As of release 51 (November 2008), Ensembl fully supports 45 species, and three additional species have preliminary support. New species in the past year include orangutan and six additional low coverage mammalian genomes. Major additions and improvements to Ensembl since our previous report include a major redesign of our website; generation of multiple genome alignments and ancestral sequences using the new Enredo-Pecan-Ortheus pipeline and development of our software infrastructure, particularly to support the Ensembl Genomes project (http://www.ensemblgenomes.org/). \u00a9 2008 The Author(s).","authors":[{"name":"Aken B.L."},{"name":"Ayling S."},{"name":"Ballester B."},{"name":"Beal K."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Bragin E."},{"name":"Brent S."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clapham P."},{"name":"Clarke L."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Curwen V."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Fairley S."},{"name":"Fernandez-Banet J."},{"name":"Fernandez-Suarez X.M."},{"name":"Fitzgerald S."},{"name":"Flicek P."},{"name":"Gordon L."},{"name":"Graf S."},{"name":"Haider S."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Herrero J."},{"name":"Holland R."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Hubbard T.J.P."},{"name":"Jenkinson A."},{"name":"Johnson N."},{"name":"Kahari A."},{"name":"Kasprzyk A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Keenan S."},{"name":"Kinsella R."},{"name":"Kokocinski F."},{"name":"Kulesha E."},{"name":"Lawson D."},{"name":"Longden I."},{"name":"Megy K."},{"name":"Meidl P."},{"name":"Overduin B."},{"name":"Parker A."},{"name":"Pritchard B."},{"name":"Proctor G."},{"name":"Rios D."},{"name":"Schuster M."},{"name":"Searle S."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smedley D."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spooner W."},{"name":"Spudich G."},{"name":"Trevanion S."},{"name":"Vilella A."},{"name":"Vogel J."},{"name":"White S."},{"name":"Wilder S."},{"name":"Zadissa A."}],"citationCount":651,"date":"2009-01-09T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Ensembl 2009"},"pmcid":"PMC2686571","pmid":"19033362","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkp972","metadata":{"abstract":"Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org) integrates genomic information for a comprehensive set of chordate genomes with a particular focus on resources for human, mouse, rat, zebrafish and other high-value sequenced genomes. We provide complete gene annotations for all supported species in addition to specific resources that target genome variation, function and evolution. Ensembl data is accessible in a variety of formats including via our genome browser, API and BioMart. This year marks the tenth anniversary of Ensembl and in that time the project has grown with advances in genome technology. As of release 56 (September 2009), Ensembl supports 51 species including marmoset, pig, zebra finch, lizard, gorilla and wallaby, which were added in the past year. Major additions and improvements to Ensembl since our previous report include the incorporation of the human GRCh37 assembly, enhanced visualisation and data-mining options for the Ensembl regulatory features and continued development of our software infrastructure. \u00a9 The Author(s) 2009. Published by Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Aken B.L."},{"name":"Ballester B."},{"name":"Beal K."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Bragin E."},{"name":"Brent S."},{"name":"Chen Y."},{"name":"Clapham P."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Dunham I."},{"name":"Durbin R."},{"name":"Fairley S."},{"name":"Fernandez-Banet J."},{"name":"Fernandez-Suarez X.M."},{"name":"Fitzgerald S."},{"name":"Flicek P."},{"name":"Gordon L."},{"name":"Graf S."},{"name":"Haider S."},{"name":"Hammond M."},{"name":"Herrero J."},{"name":"Howe K."},{"name":"Hubbard T.J.P."},{"name":"Jenkinson A."},{"name":"Johnson N."},{"name":"Kahari A."},{"name":"Keefe D."},{"name":"Keenan S."},{"name":"Kinsella R."},{"name":"Kokocinski F."},{"name":"Koscielny G."},{"name":"Kulesha E."},{"name":"Lawson D."},{"name":"Longden I."},{"name":"Massingham T."},{"name":"Mclaren W."},{"name":"Megy K."},{"name":"Overduin B."},{"name":"Parker A."},{"name":"Pritchard B."},{"name":"Proctor G."},{"name":"Rios D."},{"name":"Ruffier M."},{"name":"Schuster M."},{"name":"Searle S.M.J."},{"name":"Slater G."},{"name":"Smedley D."},{"name":"Smith J."},{"name":"Spudich G."},{"name":"Tang Y.A."},{"name":"Trevanion S."},{"name":"Vilella A."},{"name":"Vogel J."},{"name":"White S."},{"name":"Wilder S.P."},{"name":"Zadissa A."}],"citationCount":239,"date":"2009-11-11T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Ensembl's 10th year"},"pmcid":"PMC2808936","pmid":"19906699","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gku1010","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 The Author(s) 2014.Ensembl (http://www.ensembl.org) is a genomic interpretation system providing the most up-to-date annotations, querying tools and access methods for chordates and key model organisms. This year we released updated annotation (gene models, comparative genomics, regulatory regions and variation) on the new human assembly, GRCh38, although we continue to support researchers using the GRCh37.p13 assembly through a dedicated site (http://grch37. ensembl.org). Our Regulatory Build has been revamped to identify regulatory regions of interest and to efficiently highlight their activity across disparate epigenetic data sets. A number of new interfaces allow users to perform large-scale comparisons of their data against our annotations. The REST server (http://rest.ensembl.org), which allows programs written in any language to query our databases, has moved to a full service alongside our upgraded website tools. Our online Variant Effect Predictor tool has been updated to process more variants and calculate summary statistics. Lastly, the WiggleTools package enables users to summarize large collections of data sets and view them as single tracks in Ensembl. The Ensembl code base itself is more accessible: it is now hosted on our GitHub organization page (https://github.com/Ensembl) under an Apache 2.0 open source license.","authors":[{"name":"Aken B.L."},{"name":"Amode M.R."},{"name":"Barrell D."},{"name":"Beal K."},{"name":"Billis K."},{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Brent S."},{"name":"Carvalho-Silva D."},{"name":"Clapham P."},{"name":"Coates G."},{"name":"Cunningham F."},{"name":"Fitzgerald S."},{"name":"Flicek P."},{"name":"Gil L."},{"name":"Giron C.G."},{"name":"Gordon L."},{"name":"Harrow J."},{"name":"Hourlier T."},{"name":"Hunt S.E."},{"name":"Janacek S.H."},{"name":"Johnson N."},{"name":"Juettemann T."},{"name":"Kahari A.K."},{"name":"Keenan S."},{"name":"Kinsella R."},{"name":"Martin F.J."},{"name":"Maurel T."},{"name":"McLaren W."},{"name":"Muffato M."},{"name":"Murphy D.N."},{"name":"Nag R."},{"name":"Overduin B."},{"name":"Parker A."},{"name":"Patricio M."},{"name":"Perry E."},{"name":"Pignatelli M."},{"name":"Riat H.S."},{"name":"Ruffier M."},{"name":"Searle S.M.J."},{"name":"Sheppard D."},{"name":"Spudich G."},{"name":"Taylor K."},{"name":"Thormann A."},{"name":"Trevanion S.J."},{"name":"Vullo A."},{"name":"Wilder S.P."},{"name":"Yates A."},{"name":"Zadissa A."},{"name":"Zerbino D.R."}],"citationCount":897,"date":"2015-01-28T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nu